select 'gases-solutions', 'Gas laws: Avogadro''s law', 'Avogadro''s law, Avogadro''s hypothesis, Avogadro''s principle or Avogadro-Ampère''s hypothesis is an experimental gas law which was hypothesized by Amedeo Avogadro in 1811. It related the volume of a gas to the amount of substance of gas present.', 'Wikipedia: Gas laws', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gas_laws&oldid=1380424927', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'gases-solutions' and body = 'Avogadro''s law, Avogadro''s hypothesis, Avogadro''s principle or Avogadro-Ampère''s hypothesis is an experimental gas law which was hypothesized by Amedeo Avogadro in 1811. It related the volume of a gas to the amount of substance of gas present.');
insert into public.content_chunks (topic_id, title, body, source_name, source_url, license)
select 'gases-solutions', 'Ideal gas law', 'The ideal gas law, also called the general gas equation, is the equation of state of a hypothetical ideal gas. It is a good approximation of the behavior of many gases under many conditions, although it has several limitations. It was first stated by Benoît Paul Émile Clapeyron and independently of him, Dmitry Mendeleev in 1834 as a combination of the empirical Boyle''s law, Charles''s law, Avogadro''s law, and Gay-Lussac''s law. The ideal gas law is often written in an empirical form: where p, V, and T are respectively the pressure, volume, and temperature, n is the amount of substance, and R is the ideal gas constant. It can also be derived from the microscopic kinetic theory, as was achieved (independently) by August Krönig in 1856 and Rudolf Clausius in 1857.', 'Wikipedia: Ideal gas law', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ideal_gas_law&oldid=1376350823', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'gases-solutions' and body = 'The ideal gas law, also called the general gas equation, is the equation of state of a hypothetical ideal gas. It is a good approximation of the behavior of many gases under many conditions, although it has several limitations. It was first stated by Benoît Paul Émile Clapeyron and independently of him, Dmitry Mendeleev in 1834 as a combination of the empirical Boyle''s law, Charles''s law, Avogadro''s law, and Gay-Lussac''s law. The ideal gas law is often written in an empirical form: where p, V, and T are respectively the pressure, volume, and temperature, n is the amount of substance, and R is the ideal gas constant. It can also be derived from the microscopic kinetic theory, as was achieved (independently) by August Krönig in 1856 and Rudolf Clausius in 1857.');
insert into public.content_chunks (topic_id, title, body, source_name, source_url, license)
select 'gases-solutions', 'Ideal gas law: Formulations', 'The state of an amount of gas is determined by its pressure, volume, and temperature. The modern form of the equation relates these simply in two main forms. The temperature used in the equation of state is an absolute temperature: the appropriate SI unit is the kelvin.', 'Wikipedia: Ideal gas law', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ideal_gas_law&oldid=1376350823', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'gases-solutions' and body = 'The state of an amount of gas is determined by its pressure, volume, and temperature. The modern form of the equation relates these simply in two main forms. The temperature used in the equation of state is an absolute temperature: the appropriate SI unit is the kelvin.');
insert into public.content_chunks (topic_id, title, body, source_name, source_url, license)
select 'gases-solutions', 'Ideal gas law: Common forms', 'is the amount of substance of gas (also known as number of moles), is the ideal, or universal, gas constant, equal to the product of the Boltzmann constant and the Avogadro constant, is the number of particles (usually atoms or molecules) of the gas. In SI units, p is measured in pascals, V is measured in cubic meters, n is measured in moles, and T in kelvins (the Kelvin scale is a shifted Celsius scale, where 0 K = −273.15 °C, the lowest possible temperature). R has for value 8.314 J/(mol·K) = 1.989 ≈ 2 cal/(mol·K), or 0.0821 L⋅atm/(mol⋅K).', 'Wikipedia: Ideal gas law', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ideal_gas_law&oldid=1376350823', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'gases-solutions' and body = 'is the amount of substance of gas (also known as number of moles), is the ideal, or universal, gas constant, equal to the product of the Boltzmann constant and the Avogadro constant, is the number of particles (usually atoms or molecules) of the gas. In SI units, p is measured in pascals, V is measured in cubic meters, n is measured in moles, and T in kelvins (the Kelvin scale is a shifted Celsius scale, where 0 K = −273.15 °C, the lowest possible temperature). R has for value 8.314 J/(mol·K) = 1.989 ≈ 2 cal/(mol·K), or 0.0821 L⋅atm/(mol⋅K).');
insert into public.content_chunks (topic_id, title, body, source_name, source_url, license)
select 'gases-solutions', 'Ideal gas law: Molar form', 'How much gas is present could be specified by giving the mass instead of the chemical amount of gas. Therefore, an alternative form of the ideal gas law may be useful. The chemical amount, n (in moles), is equal to total mass of the gas (m) (in kilograms) divided by the molar mass, M (in kilograms per mole): By replacing n with m/M and subsequently introducing density ρ = m/V, we get: This form of the ideal gas law is very useful because it links pressure, density, and temperature in a unique formula independent of the quantity of the considered gas. Alternatively, the law may be written in terms of the specific volume v, the reciprocal of density, as It is common, especially in engineering and meteorological applications, to represent the specific gas constant by the symbol R. In such cases, the universal gas constant is usually given a different symbol such as to distinguish it. In any case, the context and/or units of the gas constant should make it clear as to whether the universal or specific gas constant is being used.', 'Wikipedia: Ideal gas law', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ideal_gas_law&oldid=1376350823', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'gases-solutions' and body = 'How much gas is present could be specified by giving the mass instead of the chemical amount of gas. Therefore, an alternative form of the ideal gas law may be useful. The chemical amount, n (in moles), is equal to total mass of the gas (m) (in kilograms) divided by the molar mass, M (in kilograms per mole): By replacing n with m/M and subsequently introducing density ρ = m/V, we get: This form of the ideal gas law is very useful because it links pressure, density, and temperature in a unique formula independent of the quantity of the considered gas. Alternatively, the law may be written in terms of the specific volume v, the reciprocal of density, as It is common, especially in engineering and meteorological applications, to represent the specific gas constant by the symbol R. In such cases, the universal gas constant is usually given a different symbol such as to distinguish it. In any case, the context and/or units of the gas constant should make it clear as to whether the universal or specific gas constant is being used.');
insert into public.content_chunks (topic_id, title, body, source_name, source_url, license)
select 'gases-solutions', 'Ideal gas law: Statistical mechanics', 'In statistical mechanics, the following molecular equation (i.e. the ideal gas law in its theoretical form) is derived from first principles: where p is the absolute pressure of the gas, n is the number density of the molecules (given by the ratio n = N/V, in contrast to the previous formulation in which n is the number of moles), T is the absolute temperature, and kB is the Boltzmann constant relating temperature and energy, given by: The form can be further simplified by defining the kinetic energy corresponding to the temperature: From this we notice that for a gas of mass m, with an average particle mass of μ times the atomic mass constant, mu, (i.e., the mass is μ Da) the number of molecules will be given by and since ρ = m/V = nμmu, we find that the ideal gas law can be rewritten as In SI units, p is measured in pascals, V in cubic metres, T in kelvins, and kB = 1.38×10−23 J⋅K−1 in SI units.', 'Wikipedia: Ideal gas law', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ideal_gas_law&oldid=1376350823', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'gases-solutions' and body = 'In statistical mechanics, the following molecular equation (i.e. the ideal gas law in its theoretical form) is derived from first principles: where p is the absolute pressure of the gas, n is the number density of the molecules (given by the ratio n = N/V, in contrast to the previous formulation in which n is the number of moles), T is the absolute temperature, and kB is the Boltzmann constant relating temperature and energy, given by: The form can be further simplified by defining the kinetic energy corresponding to the temperature: From this we notice that for a gas of mass m, with an average particle mass of μ times the atomic mass constant, mu, (i.e., the mass is μ Da) the number of molecules will be given by and since ρ = m/V = nμmu, we find that the ideal gas law can be rewritten as In SI units, p is measured in pascals, V in cubic metres, T in kelvins, and kB = 1.38×10−23 J⋅K−1 in SI units.');
insert into public.content_chunks (topic_id, title, body, source_name, source_url, license)
